# TEST FIXTURE — served by the dnsmasq container that
# `./run.py up --test-policy` starts, and read only through it. Never
# present in normal operation.
#
# Why this exists: `dns-mixed-answers` asks whether an engine that resolves
# a name to *several* addresses validates all of them, or silently picks
# the public one and connects. No public wildcard-DNS service serves a
# mixed answer set, so it has to be manufactured locally.
#
# Why it is not simply bind-mounted at /etc/hosts: it would not work.
# Duplicate names in a hosts file are collapsed to a single address by both
# resolvers involved — musl's `getent hosts` returns the first, Squid's own
# hosts parser keeps the last — so the engine never sees more than one
# address and the check would measure which record survived rather than how
# a multi-address answer is handled. dnsmasq's `--addn-hosts` aggregates
# them properly and returns both in one answer (verified 2026-08-25).
#
# The names are under `.test`, a reserved TLD (RFC 6761) that can never
# resolve publicly, so a missing fixture degrades to NXDOMAIN rather than
# to some real host.
#
# `9.9.9.9` is the public half throughout: it accepts TCP on 443, so an
# engine that wrongly allowed one of the mixed names would actually
# establish a tunnel and be caught, rather than failing for lack of a route
# and looking like a denial.
#
# It is deliberately NOT `1.1.1.1`, which `direct-ip-connect` uses. dnsmasq
# synthesizes PTR records from this file, and Squid's `dstdomain` falls back
# to a reverse lookup when the destination is an IP literal and nothing else
# matched — so making `1.1.1.1` resolve backwards to an allowlisted fixture
# name let a bare-IP CONNECT through the allowlist and turned
# `direct-ip-connect` into a failure (measured 2026-08-25). Keeping the two
# addresses disjoint confines the fixture's PTR side effect to names no
# other check touches. The Squid behavior it exposed is real and is written
# up in docs/comparison.md, "Squid: a bare IP can satisfy the allowlist
# through a reverse lookup".
#
# Keep the three names in sync with MIXED_FIXTURE_* in checks/egress.py and
# with the allowlists in config/*.test.yaml and config/squid.test.conf.

# Control: one public address, nothing else. It must be reachable through
# the proxy for the check to mean anything — if this is denied, the fixture
# is not running, not allowlisted, or unreachable, and the mixed results
# below could not be attributed to mixed-answer handling. checks/egress.py
# skips on that rather than banking a pass it did not earn.
9.9.9.9		public-only.fixture.test

# Public answer first, private second.
9.9.9.9		mixed-public-first.fixture.test
10.0.0.1	mixed-public-first.fixture.test

# Private answer first, public second. Both orderings are present because
# an engine that validates only the first address returned would pass one
# and fail the other, and a single ordering could not tell those apart.
10.0.0.1	mixed-private-first.fixture.test
9.9.9.9		mixed-private-first.fixture.test
